# The Standing Order at the Florist

Frank Ebbert put on his brown corduroy jacket at eight-oh-two, tucked a folded twenty into the small leather billfold Judith gave him, and pulled the front door closed behind him with the same three-quarter turn of the key he has used since the November of 1976.

Franklin Marshall "Frank" Ebbert — Bellefonte Area High class of 1965, Penn State '69 (secondary education, with a minor in psychology under a professor named Warren Slater whose reading list on adolescent counseling Frank still keeps in the small file cabinet in the back bedroom), guidance counselor at Bellefonte Area High School on Bishop Street from the September of 1971 until his retirement on the last Friday of May, 2012 — has lived at 314 Curtin Street in Bellefonte, Pennsylvania, in a small brick colonial with dark green shutters and a slate front step his son Peter re-set the summer of 2015, since a warm Saturday afternoon in the September of 1976, when he and his wife Judith Ann Ebbert — Bloomsburg State '69 (elementary education), first-grade teacher at Benner Elementary from the September of 1969 until her retirement in the June of 2011 — signed the deed at the Centre County Courthouse across the diamond and drove the four blocks home in the green 1974 Chevy Nova his brother-in-law had sold them for twelve hundred dollars in the July.

Judith passed on the last Thursday of October, 2016, at seven-fifty-two in the evening, in the small east-facing room on the fourth floor of Mount Nittany Medical Center in State College, of the pancreatic cancer Dr. Elaine Prokopchak — a warm and unflinching woman Frank still, on Thanksgiving, mails a card to — had told them, in the June, was not going to see the trees turn. Rebecca Lynn Cavanaugh, RN, from the hospital's oncology unit, was in the room. Frank was in the room. Their old brown mutt Duncan, twelve, was at home, on the braided rug in the front room, waiting.

The funeral was on the following Monday at St. John Catholic Church on East Bishop Street. Father Michael Rosenkrans celebrated. The reception was in the parish hall, on the folding tables the Altar and Rosary Society has been setting up in that hall since the summer of 1961.

On the first Thursday of November, at eight-oh-eight in the morning, Frank Ebbert walked the six blocks from Curtin Street to the small blue-painted flower shop on the corner of Allegheny and High, opened the door, and ordered a small hand-tied bouquet of white cushion mums.

He has done it every Thursday since.

## What the notebook on the top shelf knows

Danielle Rossi has been the owner of Bellefonte Flower Shop since the March of 2004.

She is fifty-one years old. She wears the same green canvas apron she has worn since her father Anthony handed her the keys on a Tuesday morning at eight-fifteen and drove down to Sarasota with his wife Marianne in a used Winnebago that same weekend. She keeps the shop's books in a small navy-blue quadrille notebook on the top shelf above the ribbon spools. Not because she needs to — QuickBooks does the taxes — but because her father did, in the same kind of notebook, since 1979, and because a small handwritten line in a plain notebook is the only place certain things about a small town can be honestly kept.

On the second page of the notebook, in a section she has labeled, in her own round hand, *standing*, are eleven names.

Eleven regular customers who order the same small thing every week — the widow on Linn Street who buys pink alstroemeria on Fridays for the front hall of her mother's memory-care room at Centre Crest; the third-grade teacher at Marion-Walker Elementary who buys a single yellow gerbera every Wednesday for the small vase on the desk of the guidance counselor whose husband had a heart attack in the June; the sacristan of Second Presbyterian, who mails a check on the twentieth of every month for the altar arrangements Danielle drops off on Saturday afternoons.

The first name on the list is Frank Ebbert.

*Ebbert, F. — Thurs 8:10, white cush mums, brown paper, $18 cash — for Judy.*

The line has been on that page since the eleventh of November, 2016.

Underneath the line, in the small careful hand Danielle uses when she does not want the customer to see her writing it down, are twenty-two smaller lines — one for each October since 2017 that Frank has ordered, on the second Thursday of the month, a second, larger arrangement of white and lavender chrysanthemums, wrapped the same way, paid the same way, delivered by Frank himself, on foot, to the small memorial garden at the northeast corner of the oncology unit at Mount Nittany, no card, no note, no receipt.

The last line, from the second Thursday of October, 2025, reads *large ar. — $68 cash — F. asked again about the purple African violet.*

Nobody at the hospital foundation, on the fourth floor of the converted stone building on West College Avenue, has ever seen the notebook.

## What the fourth-floor file says

On the fourth floor of the converted stone building on West College Avenue, on the same Thursday morning at eight-forty-nine, the director of individual giving at the Mount Nittany Medical Center Foundation — a bright and diligent thirty-six-year-old named Meredith Vlasich, hired in the January of 2024 from the development office at a small community hospital foundation outside Lancaster — was opening the second-quarter renewal file on a laptop at a plain oak desk under a window that faces the Nittany Ridge.

The line for Frank Ebbert came up on the sixty-fourth row down.

*EBBERT, FRANKLIN M. — 314 Curtin St., Bellefonte PA 16823. Sustaining $50/mo since 12/2016. Initial memorial gift $500 (Judith A. Ebbert, dec. 10/2016). Mail only. Spouse deceased. No engagement noted. Wealth screen: middle band. Do not upgrade.*

Meredith has never met Frank.

Meredith has never seen the small hand-tied bouquet of white cushion mums on the passenger seat of a green Chevy Nova pulling out of the small crushed-stone lot behind the flower shop at eight-eighteen on a Thursday morning in the last week of July.

Meredith has never stood at the northeast corner of the oncology-unit courtyard, on the second Thursday of an October, and watched a seventy-nine-year-old man in a brown corduroy jacket set a larger arrangement on the low stone bench beside the small purple African violet Rebecca Cavanaugh's mother-in-law started for the nurses' station in the spring of 2018.

The wealth screen has never opened the notebook on the top shelf above the ribbon spools.

## What twenty years of software has quietly missed

For twenty years, every honest hospital foundation in America has been buying — one annual contract at a time, at prices that would fund the northeast-corner memorial garden's perennial plantings for the next fifteen springs — tools that were built to answer the wrong question about Frank Ebbert.

The tools were built to answer: *of our forty-two thousand donor records, which ones look most like the ones who wrote the largest checks last year?*

Frank does not look like anyone who wrote a large check last year.

Frank has been asking a different question — quietly, on foot, every Thursday morning at eight-oh-eight for nine years and forty-one weeks — from the sidewalk between Curtin and Allegheny. His question is: *what small careful thing can I do this week, in Judy's name, that keeps her in the room?*

That is a different question. It requires a different instrument.

The instrument that could answer Frank's question is not a wealth screen. It is a small careful set of notes, assembled by someone who has spent a quiet Wednesday morning listening — to the *Centre Daily Times* obituary from the last week of October, 2016, to the two small mentions of *the Ebbert family* in the *In Memoriam* section of the hospital's annual gratitude report for 2017 and 2019, to the parish bulletin from St. John Catholic Church that lists, on the third Sunday of every October, *altar flowers this week given in loving memory of Judith Ann Ebbert, by her husband Frank*, to the small three-line note the *Centre Daily Times* ran on the eleventh of July, 2022, when the Ebbert family — Frank and his son Peter and his daughter-in-law Christine — sold Judith's grandfather's small dairy farm on Fisherman's Paradise Road for one million four hundred thousand dollars to a young Amish family from Belleville — and set them beside Frank's file, and hand them to Meredith on a Monday morning at eight-fifty, before the second-quarter portfolio meeting.

The notes would say:

*Ebbert, Franklin M. Retired guidance counselor, Bellefonte Area High School, 1971–2012. Widower of Judith A. Ebbert, deceased October 2016 in the oncology unit under Dr. Elaine Prokopchak. On every Thursday morning at eight-oh-eight since November 2016, Frank has walked from 314 Curtin to Bellefonte Flower Shop and ordered a small bouquet of white cushion mums for Judith's grave at Union Cemetery. On the second Thursday of every October since 2017, he has additionally purchased a larger arrangement, which he delivers on foot to the memorial garden at the northeast corner of the oncology unit — a gift the foundation has not, in nine years, recorded. In July 2022, the family sold Judith's grandfather's dairy farm in Bellefonte for $1.4M; the proceeds are held in a Vanguard account Frank has not touched. Suggestion, this October: no ask. A handwritten letter from Rebecca Cavanaugh, RN, retired, of the oncology unit — thanking Frank, by name, for nine October gardens, and mentioning that the small purple African violet Rebecca's mother-in-law started for the nurses' station in the spring of 2018 is still on the desk. Mail it from Bellefonte, not State College. Frank will keep it in the small oak desk in the front parlor, beside the November 2011 anniversary card Judith wrote inside the leather billfold for their fortieth.*

Meredith, on a Monday morning at eight-fifty, reads the notes. She looks at them for a long moment. She writes to Rebecca that afternoon.

That is the whole product.

## We are not the Thursday morning

Rōmy is not, and cannot be, the walk from Curtin to Allegheny at eight-oh-eight.

The bouquet is Frank's. The brown butcher paper is Frank's. The small folded twenty in the leather billfold Judith gave him for their fortieth is Frank's. The purple African violet on the desk at the nurses' station is Rebecca's mother-in-law's. The small careful line on the second page of the navy-blue quadrille notebook — *Ebbert, F. — Thurs 8:10, white cush mums, brown paper, $18 cash — for Judy* — is Danielle's, and it will stay Danielle's, and it is the whole gift, and the whole reason the second, larger October arrangement will come again this year.

What a tool can do — quietly, on the fourth floor of a converted stone building on West College Avenue on a Monday morning at eight-fifty, twenty minutes before Meredith opens the CRM — is hand her the small careful page a good listener would have written if a good listener had been keeping the shop books on Allegheny Street since 2004.

That is a smaller job than the industry has been trying to sell for twenty years.

It is also, we think, with some love, the whole job.

## A small assignment, with love

Walk into the small florist in your service area this week. Not the online one, with the fifty-dollar minimum and the same-day delivery on the app. The other one. The one with the green canvas apron, the ribbon spools on the top shelf, and the small handwritten card taped to the register that reads *no substitutions in November — please call first.*

Ask her, when the shop is quiet — after the Wednesday-morning wedding pickup and before the Thursday-afternoon funeral drop-off — how many standing orders she has.

Ask her, if she will tell you, which are the widowers.

Then, on Monday morning, before you open the CRM — before the mail-merge window suggests a fifty-dollar-a-month sustainer on the sixty-fourth row down — pull down a plain notecard from the top drawer, and write, in your own hand, the two sentences a Thursday-morning florist would have written for you, if she were paid, in this life, to write them.

Sign it.

Mail it from your kitchen table on Saturday morning, so the postmark reads the small town your donor's wife is buried in.

The green Nova is still in the drive at 314 Curtin.

The bouquet is still on the passenger seat.

Danielle has been keeping the notebook since 2004. ♡
